Tinea corporis / Ringworm (adult)
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Annular scaly plaques, Central clearing, Advancing active border

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-041
Reason for Visit: Rapidly expanding, intensely itchy annular rash causing severe sleep disturbance and anxiety about an aggressive skin infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented an acute, rapidly expanding, intensely pruritic annular skin eruption that the patient feared was the early stage of a serious systemic infection or Lyme disease. The severe itch had prevented sleep for 48 hours. Urgent out-of-hours assessment was required to definitively diagnose the rash, rule out erythema migrans or an evolving severe drug eruption, and provide immediate topical treatment and symptom relief before the next available routine appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I have these huge, spreading red rings all over my stomach and arms. They itch so intensely I can't sleep, and they are growing bigger every day."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the onset of several small, itchy red spots 7 days ago. Over the past week, these have rapidly evolved into annular erythematous plaques with a prominent scaly advancing border and distinct central clearing. The rash has an asymmetrical distribution on the glabrous skin of the trunk and left arm. The patient describes intense localized pruritus that worsens at night and when sweating. They report outward centrifugal expansion of the lesions over time, with some confluent lesions now creating polycyclic configurations. The patient recently adopted a rescue kitten 2 weeks ago, a highly probable source of contact with infected animals. The patient is anxious regarding the rapid spread.
Functional Status: Independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Sleep deprivation due to severe pruritus.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 74 Kg
General: Alert, visibly uncomfortable, frequently scratching the abdomen.
Physical Exam: On the anterior abdomen and left forearm, there are multiple 3-6 cm annular, erythematous scaly plaques. The lesions demonstrate clear central clearing with a visibly raised, intensely red, and scaly advancing active border. Scattered micro-pustules are present at the active margins of the largest abdominal plaque. The skin inside the rings is slightly hyperpigmented but completely smooth. There is no central targetoid blistering (ruling out erythema multiforme) and no deep induration or warmth (ruling out cellulitis). No generalized lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: A kitten is present in the home; scratching posts and toys visible.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient applied OTC hydrocortisone for 2 days, which exacerbated the spread (tinea incognito effect).

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Tinea Corporis (Ringworm) - Status: Acute Exacerbation (Topical steroid aggravated)
Differentials:

Erythema Migrans (Lyme Disease) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a prominent scaly border and intense pruritus; erythema migrans is typically a smooth, non-scaly, expanding targetoid rash that is largely asymptomatic.

Nummular Eczema - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinct central clearing; nummular eczema typically presents as uniformly inflamed, coin-shaped, crusted/scaly plaques without a clear center.

Pityriasis Rosea - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of pustules on the advancing margin, asymmetrical distribution, and clear animal contact history.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with classical Tinea Corporis, characterized by highly pruritic, expanding annular plaques with central clearing, directly linked to recent animal contact. Triage correctly identified the severe itch and rapid expansion as requiring assessment to rule out more serious annular rashes (e.g., Lyme disease). The patient's inadvertent use of over-the-counter hydrocortisone has suppressed local immunity, accelerating the outward centrifugal expansion (tinea incognito). Immediate out-of-hours evaluation was critical to correctly diagnose the fungal etiology, instruct the immediate cessation of the topical steroid, and prescribe targeted fungicidal therapy to halt the rapid spread and relieve the intense pruritus.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed immediate cessation of OTC hydrocortisone cream. Prescribed topical Terbinafine 1% cream to be applied BD to the lesions and 2 cm beyond the visible border. Advised an oral antihistamine (Cetirizine 10mg) for nocturnal itch control.
Positive Behavioral Change: By strictly applying the anti-fungal cream two centimeters past the red ring, you will actively kill the invisible fungus that is marching outward, stopping the rings from growing any larger. By completely avoiding steroid creams on these spots, you will allow your skin's natural immune system to fight back, rapidly clearing the infection.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to ensure complete resolution. Recommend veterinary check for the kitten.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the red rings become deeply hot, swollen, or begin weeping thick yellow pus, or if you develop a high fever, present to a medical centre or the Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Fungal infections require consistent treatment well past the point when the skin looks normal to prevent recurrence.
Clinical Course: The intense itching will stop within 3-4 days on the anti-fungal cream. The red rings will slowly fade over 2-4 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Continue the cream for exactly 7 days after the skin appears completely healed to eradicate all microscopic spores.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You have contracted a common fungal infection from your new kitten. The fungus lives on the dead top layer of skin and grows outward in a ring. The steroid cream you used accidentally fed the fungus. The new anti-fungal cream will directly poison the fungus.
Actionable Steps: Wash your hands thoroughly after touching the kitten or applying the cream. Do not share towels or clothing. Have the kitten checked by a vet.

